[h2]Heroes aren't born. They survive.[/h2]
Exiled from the empire, you reach a remote island haunted by monsters with a handful of villagers and little else. Wanderlust: Triumph is a roguelite strategy RPG about the people who live and die out there: the villagers who take the jobs nobody else will, and the rare few who survive long enough to become heroes. You decide who goes, who stays, and what each of them is worth risking.

[img]{STEAM_CLAN_IMAGE}/about-anim-map.gif[/img]

[h2]Quests arrive as offers[/h2]
Work reaches your island as offers, not missions. Each one declares its danger, its reward tier, and how long it stays open. Some wait for days; others expire while your best people are still in the field. You are never told the whole story — just enough to decide what your people can afford to pay.

[h2]Every quest is a step on a ladder[/h2]
The quests with the best rewards demand more than you have. Take on smaller jobs to earn the skills, gear and blueprints that unlock them, then do it again one step higher. Grow strong enough to leave the busywork behind, and the island answers with harder quests and rewards worth the danger.

Every choice has to serve a goal. Facing a swarm means building area damage and the right defense, even if that is not the best pick anywhere else. Plan for the short term, the middle, and the long haul.

[h2]Read the risk. Let the Astrolabe decide.[/h2]
Before each expedition, you see a detailed forecast for the exact party you assemble. Choose which skill leads the attempt, spend consumables to tilt the odds, assign a bodyguard to take the blows meant for someone else — then let the Astrolabe decide: five possible outcomes, from triumph to disaster. Your choices shape the odds; the roll decides your fate.

[img]{STEAM_CLAN_IMAGE}/about-anim-astrolabe.gif[/img]

[h2]From one hero to a company of legends[/h2]
You begin with a single party sent on one quest at a time. Over time you field several teams, and eventually an elite company of heroes ready for heroic quests. Heroes begin as blank slates: the skills, spells, equipment and stats you find shape each one into a different build, and slowly an identity of their own.

[h2]Villagers die. A few become legends.[/h2]
Villagers are fragile and plentiful. Those who survive long enough face the Trial of Fire, and an ordinary villager can ascend to the rank of hero. The island is not kind: villagers with little chance of rising may not live to try, and losing a hero means losing the skills, traits and potential they carried.

[h2]Fall. Learn. Come back stronger.[/h2]
When your settlement falls, the run ends, but what you earned carries forward: blueprints, rewards, and bonuses from the buildings you invested in. Skip the early phases and push further next time. Waves of attackers strike in cycles; defeat the bosses who command them and the world moves into a new phase.

After a few collapses, you will know the road to the mid-game. You lost this time, but you got further, and you found the missing piece of a strategy that finally looks promising.

[h2]A world that remembers[/h2]
A campaign unfolds across many runs, with authored stories, a vast number of quests with different twists, and consequences that persist. The world and the people in it, those you play and those you meet, change with what you do.

[h2]A settlement that supports them[/h2]
Between expeditions, the village is yours to run. Assign villagers to the jobs that keep everyone alive — fields, workshop, watch — and choose what to build and what to postpone: every pair of hands in the fields is one you cannot send into the wild.

Your village is the base your heroes return to. It feeds your people, supplies gear and blueprints, and unlocks new ways to grow: new spells, new equipment, new options. The story, though, is told by the people you send out, not the walls behind them.

[h2]Features[/h2]
[list]
[*] A roguelite campaign: every fallen settlement makes the next run stronger
[*] Quest offers that declare their danger, reward and expiry — and the cost of letting them lapse
[*] A village that runs on your choices: assign workers, keep them fed, build what the next expedition needs
[*] A ladder of quests, each tier unlocking the next
[*] A forecast for the exact party you send, and fate decided on the Astrolabe
[*] Party roles that matter — a bodyguard can die in a hero's place
[*] Blank-slate heroes built from the skills, spells and gear you find
[*] Villagers who can rise to heroes, and the cost when they fall
[*] Hundreds of hours of replayability across many runs
[*] A painterly, animated world of wonder and rough beauty
[*] Steam Achievements
[*] Take your time: pause whenever you need. Easy to learn, hard to master.
[/list]

[h2]In development[/h2]
Wanderlust: Triumph is being made by a solo developer. Playtests and a free demo for Steam Next Fest are planned. English and Italian are planned for launch, with Spanish, German and Chinese to follow. Modding tools are being explored.

[h2]Roadmap[/h2]
[b]Next:[/b] playtest signups and a free demo for Steam Next Fest (dates to be announced)
[b]Then:[/b] Early Access, with your feedback shaping the road to 1.0
[b]Later:[/b] more quests, heroes, spells and regions; more languages
[i]Release date: to be announced.[/i]

[h2]Help shape the game[/h2]
Wishlist and follow Wanderlust: Triumph to be notified of the playtest and the demo. When a playtest is open, use the Request Access button on this page, and tell us what worked and what didn't in the Community Hub discussions.

Forge your heroes. Brave the unknown. Turn adversity into triumph.
